![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

When validating collections of data, we should explicitly check for and record malformed entries as structured validation failures, and skip further processing of those entries to avoid runtime property access errors. Currently, `sanitizeCandidateVlmOutputRows` maps over `rows` without verifying that each entry is a non-null object, which could lead to runtime issues or malformed output objects if an entry is invalid.

```javascript
export function sanitizeCandidateVlmOutputRows(rows, diagnostics = []) {
  const valid = [];
  asArray(rows).forEach((row, index) => {
    if (!row || typeof row !== 'object' || Array.isArray(row)) {
      diagnostics.push({
        code: 'malformed_vlm_output_row',
        row_index: index,
        reason: 'row_is_not_an_object',
        severity: 'critical',
      });
      return;
    }
    valid.push({
      candidate_status: 'candidate_only_not_accepted',
      ...cleanVlmOutputObject(row, diagnostics),
    });
  });
  return valid;
}
```

